===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q11471}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[inevitable]] [[progression]] into the [[future]] with the passing of [[present]] and [[past]] events.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:time}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Time''' stops for nobody.&nbsp;&nbsp; the ebb and flow of '''time'''}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1937|author=w:Delmore Schwartz|url=http://www.americanpoems.com/poets/Delmore-Schwartz/3856|title=Calmly We Walk Through This April's Day
|passage='''Time''' is the fire in which we burn.}}
#* {{quote-av
|en
|date=May 26, 2023
|author=w:Volodymyr Zelenskyy
|title=Zelenskyy surprises the Johns Hopkins commencement ceremony
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230526060203/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m8Fy5fQz9Rs
|publisher=w:MSNBC
|time=0:40
|text=One of the most common truisms on Earth is the advice to value or at least not waste '''time'''. Why has it become so widespread? Every person eventually realizes that '''time''' is the most valuable resource on the planet. Not oil or uranium. Not lithium or anything else, but '''time'''. '''Time'''. The very flow of '''time''' convinces us of this. Some people realize this sooner, and these are the lucky ones. Others realize it too late when they lose someone or something. People cannot avoid it, this is just a matter of '''time'''. But there is a fundamental difference that comes down to the question of '''time'''. The '''time''' of your life is under your control. The '''time''' of life of our force on the front line, the '''time''' of life of all Ukrainians who are forced to live through this terrible Russian aggression unfortunately is subject to many factors that are not all in their control. I do not wish anyone to feel like they are in my shoes, and it's impossible to give a manual on how to go through life so as not to waste '''time'''. However, one piece of advice always works. You have to know exactly why you need today and how you want your tomorrows to look like.}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} The feeling of the passage of events and their relative duration, as experienced by an individual.
##: {{ux|en|'''Time''' flies when you're having fun.}}
## {{lb|en|physics|usually|uncountable}} A dimension of [[spacetime]] with the opposite metric signature to [[space]] dimensions; the [[fourth dimension]].
##: {{ux|en|Both science-fiction writers and physicists have written about travel through '''time'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Wells Time Machine||31|So long as I travelled at a high velocity through '''time''', this scarcely mattered; I was, so to speak, attenuated&nbsp;– was slipping like a vapour through the interstices of intervening substances!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1999|w:Brian Greene|w:[[The Elegant Universe|The Elegant Universe: Superstrings, Hidden Dimensions, and the Quest for the Ultimate Theory]]|publisher=w:W. W. Norton & Company||isbn=9780393338102|204|year_published=2010|We all have a visceral understanding of what it means for the universe to have multiple space dimensions, since we live in a world in which we constantly deal with a plurality — three. But what would it mean to have multiple '''times'''? Would one align with '''time''' as we presently experience it psychologically while the other would somehow be "different"?}}
## {{lb|en|physics|uncountable}} Change associated with the second law of [[thermodynamics]]; the physical and psychological result of increasing [[entropy]].
##: {{ux|en|'''Time''' slows down when you approach the speed of light.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1992|w:Roger Highfield; w:Peter Coveney|Arrow of Time||179|Given the connection between increasing entropy and the arrow of '''time''', does the Big Crunch mean that '''time''' would run backwards as soon as collapse began?|publisher=w:Ballantine Books|isbn=0449907236}}
##* {{quote-book|en|2012|Robert Zwilling|Natural Sciences and Human Thought|publisher=w:Springer Science+Business Media|isbn=9783642786853||80|Eventually '''time''' would also die because no processes would continue, no light would flow.}}
## {{lb|en|physics|uncountable|reductionist definition}} The property of a system which allows it to have more than one distinct configuration.
##: {{ux|en|An essential definition of '''time''' should entail neither speed nor direction, just change.}}
# A [[duration]] of time.
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} A quantity of availability of [[duration]].
##: {{ux|en|More '''time''' is needed to complete the project.}}
##: {{ux|en|You had plenty of '''time''', but you waited until the last minute.}}
##: {{ux|en|Are you finished yet? '''Time'''’s up!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1661|w:[[John Fell (bishop)|John Fell]]|The Life of the most learned, reverend and pious Dr. H. Hammond|https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-life-of-the-most-lea_fell-john-bishop_1661|7|During the whole '''time''' of his abode in the Univerſity he generally ſpent 13 hours of the day in Study&nbsp;;{{…}}|location=w:London}}
##* {{quote-book|en|2021|w:[[Lisa Miller (psychologist)|Lisa Miller]]|The Awakened Brain|isbn=9781984855626|chapter=2||37|The teacher greeted us and moved methodically through the room, placing an object on each student's desk. It was a thin book―a spiral-bound planner. She strode to the front of the room. "If you want to succeed," she said, her syllables clear and careful, "you must learn to manage your '''time'''."}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A measurement of a quantity of time; a numerical or general indication of a length of progression.
##: {{co|en|a long '''time'''}}
##: {{ux|en|Record the individual '''times''' for the processes in each batch.}}
##: {{ux|en|Only your best '''time''' is compared with the other competitors.}}
##: {{ux|en|The algorithm runs in O(n<sup>2</sup>) '''time'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|1|1|I was about to say that I had known the Celebrity from the time he wore kilts. But I see I will have to amend that, because he was not a celebrity then, nor, indeed, did he achieve fame until some '''time''' after I left New York for the West.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1938|w:[[Richard Hughes (British writer)|Richard Hughes]]|In Hazard|https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.207695|248|publisher=w:Chatto & Windus|location=w:London|The shock of the water, of course, woke him, and he swam for quite a '''time'''.}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable|slang}} The serving of a prison sentence.
##: {{ux|en|The judge leniently granted a sentence with no hard '''time'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|He is not living at home because he is doing '''time'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1994|Dana Stabenow|A Cold-Blooded Business||64|isbn=0425158497|Arrested on duty at Fort Richardson, both parents had worked hard at blaming the other for their son’s death, but Kate’s meticulous recording of the detail of the bruising found on the child’s body and the physical evidence surrounding the scene, plus patient, painstaking interviews with neighbors above and below stairs had resulted in '''time''' for both.}}
##* {{RQ:Noire Thug-A-Licious|108|"Ain’t gone be no Rikers Island for you next time," I warned him. "You get tapped on another gun charge and you looking at some upstate '''time'''."}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} An experience.
##: {{ux|en|We had a wonderful '''time''' at the party.}}
## {{senseid|en|era}} {{lb|en|countable}} An [[era]]; (articulated, sometimes in the plural) the current era, the current state of affairs.
##: {{co|en|Roman '''times'''}}
##: {{co|en|the '''time''' of the dinosaurs}}
##: {{ux|en|That’s the way things were in those '''times'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|1|5|259|1|The '''time''' is out of ioynt: O curſed ſpight,<br>That euer I was borne to ſet it right.}}
##* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title=w:[[Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect]]|genre=science fiction|location=w:Redwood City|publisher=w:Electronic Arts|year=2008|system=PC|scene=Eden Prime|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=Dr. Manuel: You're wasting your time. The age of humanity is over. Our extinction is inevitable.[...]<br>Shepard: I don't have time for this.<br>Dr. Manuel: Time? Our '''time''' is over.}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable|with|possessive}} A person's youth or young adulthood, as opposed to the present day.
##: {{ux|en|In my '''time''', we respected our elders.}}
## {{lb|en|only in singular|sports|and|figuratively}} {{1|time out}}; temporary, limited suspension of play.
# An [[instant]] of time.
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} The duration of time of a given day that has passed; the moment, as indicated by a clock or similar device.
##: {{ux|en|Excuse me, have you got the '''time'''?}}
##: {{ux|en|What '''time''' is it, do you guess? Ten o’clock?}}
##: {{ux|en|A computer keeps '''time''' using a clock battery.}}
##: {{ux|en|They will be asleep by this '''time''', [[don'tcha]] think?}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-19||Ian Sample|Irregular bedtimes may affect children’s brains|w:The Guardian Weekly|volume=189|issue=6||34|articleurl=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2013/jul/08/irregular-bedtimes-affect-childrens-brains|Going to bed at a different '''time''' each night affected girls more than boys, but both fared worse on mental tasks than children who had a set bedtime, researchers found.}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A particular moment or hour; the appropriate moment or hour for something (especially with prepositional phrase or imperfect subjunctive).
##: {{ux|en|it’s '''time''' for (you to go to) bed;&nbsp; it’s '''time''' to sleep;&nbsp; we must wait for the right '''time''';&nbsp; it's '''time''' we were going}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|8||The humor of my proposition appealed more strongly to Miss Trevor than I had looked for, and from that '''time''' forward she became her old self again; for, even after she had conquered her love for the Celebrity, the mortification of having been jilted by him remained.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07||w:Joseph Stiglitz|Globalisation is about taxes too|w:The Guardian Weekly|volume=188|issue=26||19|articleurl=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/may/27/globalisation-is-about-taxes-too|It is '''time''' the international community faced the reality: we have an unmanageable, unfair, distortionary global tax regime.}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A numerical indication of a particular moment.
##: {{ux|en|At what '''times''' do the trains arrive?}}
##: {{ux|en|These '''times''' were erroneously converted between zones}}
## {{senseid|en|occurrence}}{{lb|en|countable}} An [[instance]] or [[occurrence]].
##: {{syn|en|instant|juncture|Thesaurus:point in time}}
##: {{coi|en|one more '''time'''}}
##: {{ux|en|When was the last '''time''' we went out? I don’t remember.}}
##: {{ux|en|See you another '''time'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|That’s three '''times''' he’s made the same mistake.}}
##: {{ux|en|Okay, but this is the last '''time'''. No more after that!}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|2|15|Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first '''time''' Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke. He was dressed out in broad gaiters and bright tweeds, like an English tourist, and his face might have belonged to Dagon, idol of the Philistines.}}
## {{lb|en|UK|in public houses}} {{1|closing time}}.
##: {{ux|en|Last call: it's almost '''time'''.}}
## The hour of [[childbirth]].
##* {{RQ:Clarendon History|VIII|370|{{…}}came well to ''Exeter''; where ſhe intended to ſtay, till ſhe was deliver’d; for ſhe was within little more than one Month of her '''time''';{{…}}}}
## {{lb|en|with|possessives}} The end of someone’s life, [[conceive]]d by the speaker as having been [[predestine]]d.
##: {{ux|en|It was his '''time'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The [[measurement]] under some [[system]] of [[region]] of [[day]] or [[moment]].
#: {{ux|en|Let's synchronize our watches so we're not on different '''time'''.}}
