##: {{ux|en|After the introduction, the drummer is to play '''time'''.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar|obsolete}} {{syn of|en|tense}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''time''' of a verb}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1823|Lindley Murray|Key to the Exercises Adapted to Murray’s English Grammar|publisher=Fortland||53f|Though we have, in the notes under the thirteenth rule of the Grammar, explained in general the principles, on which the '''time''' of a verb in the infinitive mood may be ascertained, and its form determined;{{…}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1829|Benjamin A. Gould|Adam’s Latin Grammar||153|location=w:Boston|The participles of the future '''time''' active, and perfect passive, when joined with the verb ''esse'', were sometimes used as indeclinable;{{…}}}}
# {{lb|en|slang|MLE}} {{clipping of|en|[[a]] [[long]] [[time]]}}.
#: {{syn|en|ages|long}}
#* {{quote-song|en|date=2019-09-15|artist=w:Stormzy|album=Heavy Is The Head|track=12|title=Wiley Flow|url=https://genius.com/Stormzy-wiley-flow-lyrics|passage=I used to pay for things but that was '''time''' ago.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=2022-03-18|director=Brady Hood|writer=Ronan Bennett; Gerry Jackson; Tyrone Rashard; Sagirah Gammon|actor=|role=girl called B|medium=TV|series=4|number=1|title={{w|Top Boy}}(Good Morals)|time=00:38:33|text=Ats' mum is looking for him, says he ain't been back in '''time'''}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=2023-01-15|director=Freddy Syborn|writer=Layton Williams|actor=Anthony J. Abraham|role=Inchez|medium=TV|series=4|number=3|title={{w|Bad Education}}(Prison)|time=12:51|text=INCHEZ:Man this is long! We've been in here for '''time'''!}}

====Usage notes====
* For the number of occurrences and the ratio of comparison, ''[[once]]'' and ''[[twice]]'' are typically used instead of ''one time'' and ''two times''. ''[[thrice|Thrice]]'' is uncommon in American English and British English but is still common in Asian English, for example in India, Singapore, and Malaysia.<ref>James Lambert, 2012, 'Diachronic stability in Indian English lexis' ''World Englishes'' Vol. 31, issue 1, page 112-127. [https://doi.org/10.1111/weng.12072]</ref>
* {{s|music}} The distinction between ''time'' as tempo ({{senseno|en|tempo}}) and ''time'' as meter ({{senseno|en|meter}}) is relatively modern. The dated expression "march time" refers equally to the tempo of a march as it does to the use of 2/4 or 4/4 meter.
* Ratio usage with comparatives, such as "you earn three times more than me", though common, is strictly illogical. More correctly: "you earn three times as much as me".

