# {{lb|en|impersonal}} To produce thunder; to sound, rattle, or roar, as a discharge of atmospheric electricity.
#: {{ux|en|It '''thundered''' continuously.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a [[noise]] like thunder.
#: {{ux|en|The train '''thundered''' along the tracks.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author={{w|Bill Oddie}}|title=Gone Birding|publisher=Methuen|location=London|page=59|passage=The farmer whose land the Pratincole had chosen to frequent had such an adversion to birders that he had been '''thundering''' up and down all day in a high-powered muck-spreader, splattering them with cow dung!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Abby Chava Stein|title=Becoming Eve|publisher=Seal Press|page=46|passage=His words landed like a bomb, and my ears '''thundered''' with noise.}}
# {{lb|en|ergative}} To (make something) [[move]] very [[fast]] (with loud noise).
#* {{quote-song|en|lyricist=w:David Sylvian|composer=w:Ryuichi Sakamoto|title=w:Forbidden Colours|album={{w|Merry Christmas Mr. Lawrence (soundtrack)|Merry Christmas Mr. Lawrence}}|artist=David Sylvian|year=1983|text=Senseless years '''thunder''' by / Millions are willing to give their lives for you / Does nothing live on?}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive}} To say (something) with a [[loud]], [[threatening]] voice.
#: {{ux|en|"Get back to work at once!", he '''thundered'''.}}
# To produce something with incredible power.
#* {{quote-journal|en
|date=January 19, 2011
|author=Jonathan Stevenson
|title=Leeds 1 - 3 Arsenal
|work=w:BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9364559.stm
