===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|teien}}, {{m|enm|teiȝen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tīġan}}, {{m|ang|tīeġan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*taugijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*taugijaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dewk-|t=to tug, draw}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|teygja}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[twist]] (a string, rope, or the like) around itself [[secure]]ly.
#: {{ux|en|'''Tie''' this rope in a knot for me, please.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Tie''' the rope to this tree.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To form (a knot or the like) in a string or the like.
#: {{ux|en|'''Tie''' a knot in this rope for me, please.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attach]] or [[fasten]] (one thing to another) by string or the like.
#: {{ux|en|'''Tie''' him to the tree.}}
#* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne
|passage=In bond of virtuous love together '''tied'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|sometimes|figurative}} To secure (something) by string or the like.
#: {{ux|en|'''Tie''' your shoes.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Astraea Redux
|passage=Not '''tied''' to rules of policy, you find / Revenge less sweet than a forgiving mind.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To have the [[same]] [[score]] or [[position]] as another in a [[competition]] or [[ordering]].
#: {{ux|en|They '''tied''' for third place.}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''tied''' the game.}}
# {{lb|en|US|transitive}} To have the same score or position as (another) in a competition or ordering.
#: {{ux|en|He '''tied''' me for third place.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} To unite (musical notes) with a [[line]] or [[slur]] in the [[notation]].
# {{lb|en|US|dated|colloquial}} To [[believe]]; to [[credit]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|title=Collier's|volume=84|page=56
|passage={{...}} It seems they have sort of betrothal teas — can you '''tie''' it?"<br>"Heavens!" said Mary {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1940|journal=Woman's Home Companion|volume=67|issue=1-4|page=134
|passage=As the door slammed Pete turned to Hally, fuming. "Can you '''tie''' that? A little twopenny cold frightening him off."}}
# {{lb|en|programming|transitive}} In the [[Perl]] [[programming language]], to extend (a variable) so that standard operations performed upon it [[invoke]] [[custom]] [[functionality]] instead.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Larry Wall; Tom Christiansen; Jon Orwant|title=Programming Perl: 3rd Edition|page=814
|passage=So, a class for '''tying''' a hash to an ISAM implementation might provide an extra method to traverse a set of keys sequentially (the “S” of ISAM), since your typical DBM implementation can't do that.}}

