===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|id=sensitive|:inh|enm:tender}}
From {{inh|en|enm|tender}}, {{m|enm|tendere}}, from {{der|en|xno|tendre}}, {{der|en|fro|tendre}}, from {{der|en|la|tener|tener, tenerum|t=soft, delicate}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Sensitive or [[pain|painful]] to the touch.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|III|2|text={{...}}poore Lord, is't I<br/>That chaſe thee from thy Countrie, and expoſe<br/>Thoſe '''tender''' limbes of thine{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2006|actor=Mike Myers|role=Shrek|title=Shrek|passage=Be careful: that area is '''tender'''.}}
# Easily bruised or injured; not firm or hard; [[delicate]].
#: {{ux|en|'''tender''' plants}}
#: {{ux|en|'''tender''' flesh}}
#: {{ux|en|'''tender''' fruit}}
# Physically weak; not able to [[endure]] [[hardship]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Deuteronomy|28|56|passage=the '''tender''' and delicate woman among you}}
# {{lb|en|of food}} Soft and easily chewed.
#* '''2001''', Joey Pantolino (character), ''The Matrix'' (movie)
#*: The Matrix is telling my brain this steak is '''tender''', succulent, and juicy.
# [[sensible|Sensible]] to impression and pain; easily pained.
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=Our Bodies are not naturally more '''Tender''' than our Faces.}}
# [[fond|Fond]], [[loving]], [[gentle]], or [[sweet]].
#: {{ux|en|Suzanne was such a '''tender''' mother to her children.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|II|iii|passage=You, that are thus so '''tender''' o'er his follies, <br> Will never do him good.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|James|5|11|passage=The Lord is very pitiful, and of '''tender''' mercy.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Good Thoughts in Bad Times|section=II|page=3|passage=''Lord''. VVHen thou ſhalt viſit me vvith a ſharp diſeaſe, I fear I ſhall be impatient. For I am Cholerick by my Nature, and '''tender''' by my Temper, and have not been acquainted vvith Sickneſſe all my life time.}}
# {{senseid|en|young}} [[young|Young]] and [[inexperienced]].
#: {{ux|en|I first had a girlfriend at the '''tender''' age of seven.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1970|title=w:Teach Your Children|author=w:Graham Nash|passage=And you of '''tender''' years can't know the fears that your elders grew by.}}
#* '''2001''' October 15, Appeals Court of Illinios (Second District) in ''Appelhans v. McFall'':
#*: {{quote|en|The court later expressly adopted the '''tender''' years doctrine, which states that a child is incapable of contributory negligence if he is less than seven years old{{...|. Chicago City Ry. Co. v. Tuohy, 196 Ill. 410, 422, 63 N.E. 997 (1902)}}.}}
# Adapted to excite feeling or sympathy; expressive of the softer passions; [[pathetic]].
#: {{ux|en|'''tender''' expressions; '''tender''' expostulations; a '''tender''' strain}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XX|33|The lesser griefs that may be said,<br>⁠That breathe a thousand '''tender''' vows,<br>⁠Are but as servants in a house<br>Where lies the master newly dead; […]}}
# Apt to give pain; causing grief or pain; delicate.
#: {{ux|en|a '''tender''' subject}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Cunning|passage=Things that are '''tender''' and unpleasing.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} Heeling over too easily when under sail; said of a vessel.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Exciting kind concern; dear; precious.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|V|iv|passage=I love Valentine, <br> Whose life's as '''tender''' to me as my soul!}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Careful to keep inviolate, or not to injure; used with ''of''.
#* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage='''tender''' of property}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1694|author=w:John Tillotson|title=The Advantages of Religion to Societies
|passage=The civil authority should be '''tender''' of the honour of God and religion.}}

