==English==
{{wp}}
[[Image:MENISCAS 180.jpg|thumb|right|An optical telescope.]]

===Etymology===
From {{confix|en|tele-|-scope}}.
From {{der|en|la|tēlescopium}}, from {{der|en|grc|τηλεσκόπος||far-seeing}}, from {{m|grc|τῆλε||afar}} + {{m|grc|σκοπέω||to look at}}.

Coined in 1611 by the Greek mathematician [[w:Giovanni Demisiani|Giovanni Demisiani]] for one of [[w:Galileo Galilei|Galileo Galilei]]'s instruments presented at a banquet at the [[w:Accademia dei Lincei|Accademia dei Lincei]]. {{doublet|en|Telescopium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛl.ɪ.skəʊp/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛl.əˌskoʊp/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-telescope.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|tele|scope}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[monocular]] [[optical]] [[instrument]] that [[magnify|magnifies]] distant objects, especially in [[astronomy]].
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Sartor Resartus|chapter=Symbols|page=155|passage=It needs a scientific '''telescope''', it needs to be reinterpreted and artificially brought near us, before we can so much as know that it ''was'' a Sun.}}
#* {{RQ:Darwin Origin of Species|page=188|passage=It is scarcely possible to avoid comparing the eye to a '''telescope'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Sidereal Messenger of Galileo Galilei and a Part of the Preface to Kepler's Dioptrics Containing the Original Account of Galileo's Astronomical Discoveries: A Translation with Introduction and Notes|year=1880|passage=About ten months ago a report reached my ears that a Dutchman had constructed a '''telescope''', by the aid of which visible objects, although at a great distance from the eye of the observer, were seen distinctly as if near; {{...}}|author=w:Galileo Galilei|page=10|origdate=13 March 1610|translator=Edward Stafford Carlos|chapter=The Astronomical Messenger|oclc=433105990|publisher=Rivingtons|location=London|author2=w:Johannes Kepler|original=Sidereus Nuncius: {{...}}|type=}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=w:The Universe in a Nutshell|passage=This was one of the great missed opportunities of theoretical physics. If Einstein had stuck with his original equations, he could have predicted that the universe must be either expanding or contracting. As it was, the possibility of a time-dependent universe wasn't taken seriously until observations in the 1920s by the 100-inch '''telescope''' on Mount Wilson.|author=w:Stephen Hawking|page=21|isbn=9780553802023|date=2001 November|chapter=A Brief History of Relativity|oclc=318274510}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Don Lincoln|title=The telescope that will replace Hubble will launch on Christmas. Here’s why it matters|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/12/24/opinions/james-webb-space-telescope-launch-lincoln|date=2021-12-24|passage=The 1.2-meter-diameter (4-foot-diameter) '''telescope''' has set off on a monthlong journey to its orbital destination of the sun-Earth Lagrange point L2, which is nearly 1 million miles (1.6 million kilometers) away from Earth and also home to NASA’s James Webb Space Telescope. Euclid will keep pace with Earth as our planet orbits the sun.}}
# Any instrument used in astronomy for observing distant objects (such as a [[radio telescope]]).
# {{lb|en|television}} A [[retractable]] [[tubular]] [[support]] for [[light]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1963|title=Television Engineering: Report|page=245|text=In some studios the '''telescopes''' are fixed to the lighting grid {{...}}}}
# A kind of [[goldfish]] with protruding eyes, first bred in China.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=Robert V. Sawyer; Edwin H. Perkins|title=Water Gardens and Goldfish|page=92
|passage=Chinese '''Telescopes''' are sold by some dealers for young Japanese Fringetails. For this reason it is well to select a reliable dealer when purchasing choice stock.}}

