|passage=And then she changed her voice and would be as saft as honey: 'My puir wee Ailie, was I thrawn '''till''' ye? Never mind, my bonnie. You and me are a' that's left, and we maunna be ill to ither.'}}
# {{lb|en|dialectal}} So that (something may happen).
#* '''1953?''', {{w|Samuel Beckett}}, ''{{w|Waiting for Godot}}''
#*: VLADIMIR: Together again at last! We'll have to celebrate this. But how? (He reflects.) Get up '''till''' I embrace you.

=====Usage notes=====
* The preposition {{m|en||till}} is [[ubiquitous#Adjective|ubiquitous]] in [[Appendix:Glossary#informal|informal]] [[register#English:_language_register|register]] of modern English; nonetheless, in formal register it is often replaced with {{m|en|until}} or {{m|en|to}}, except in some [[w:varieties of English|varieties]], such as [[Indian English]]. This predisposition is likely influenced by the widespread misapprehension that {{m|en||till}} is a [[Appendix:Glossary#clipping|clipping]] of {{m|en||until}}, which it is not ({{m|en||until}} being an enhanced form of {{m|en||till}}). The spelling {{m|en|'til}}, itself also deprecated by some writers, was born of that same misapprehension.

