===Preposition===
{{head|sv|preposition}}

# [[to]]
#: {{uxi|sv|Välkommen '''till''' Sverige!|Welcome '''to''' Sweden!}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Ge den '''till''' mig!|Give it '''to''' me!}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Vi behöver två '''till''' fem nya datorer|We need two '''to''' five new computers}}
# [[for]]
#: {{coi|sv|en bra TV '''till''' ett bra pris|a good TV '''for''' a good price}}
#: {{coi|sv|en present '''till''' min syster|a present '''for''' my sister}}
#: {{coi|sv|pengar '''till''' resan|money '''for''' the trip}}
#: {{uxi|sv|Vad vill du ha '''till''' middag?|What do you want '''for''' dinner?}}
# [[with]]
#: {{uxi|sv|Jag tar mjölk '''till''' mitt kaffe|I take milk '''with''' my coffee}}
# [[of]]
#: {{coi|sv|en kompis '''till''' mig|a friend '''of''' mine}}
# [[this]] (the coming) {{q|As a rule of thumb, "till" is only used for (longer) time periods that you might put in the definite, like seasons or the weekend, and not for days of the week and the like. See also {{m|sv|nu}}.}}
#: {{ux|sv|'''Till sommaren''' ska vi åka på semester till Island|'''This (coming) summer''' we are going on vacation to Iceland|footer="Ahead in the summer," intuitively. Would not be said during the summer to refer to the next summer, as "ahead in the summer" sounds weird in that case.}}
#: {{ux|sv|(Nu) '''till helgen''' avgörs Allsvenskan|The {{w|Allsvenskan}} [a soccer league] title will be decided '''this (coming) weekend'''|lit=(Now) '''this (coming) weekend''', Allsvenskan is decided/determined [{{m|sv|nu|Nu|now}} can be used to emphasize a point in time close to the present. Compare "på fredag" (on Friday) vs. "nu på fredag" (this Friday).]|footer="Ahead during the weekend," intuitively, or "ahead this weekend" with {{m|sv|nu||now}}.}}
#: {{ux|sv|Jag ska dit '''till veckan'''|I'm going [to] there [thither] '''next week''' [the coming week]|footer=Might also imply not very late next week. Could also be expressed as "Jag ska dit nästa vecka" (I'm going there next week). "Till {{l|sv|dagen||the day}} / {{l|sv|månaden||the month}} / {{l|sv|året||the year}}" and the like sound strange – "till" might mostly be limited to weeks.}}

====Usage notes====
* Often (more or less subconsciously to native speakers) clipped to "ti" (/tɪ/) in speech. Such clipping is less common for the adverb below, even when ''till'' is not the final word in the sentence, due to ''till'' being stressed as an adverb.
* Earlier, ''till'' governed the genitive case. Remains can still be found in certain expressions:
{{col|sv|n=4|ge sig till tåls<t:show patience>|tillbaka<t:back>|till bords<t:at the table>|till buds<t:at one's disposal>|dom<alt:till doms><t:to judgement>|till döds<t:to death>|fred<alt:tillfreds><t:at peace, content>|till godo<t:for good, as credit>|till fots<t:on foot>|till hands<t:at hand>|tillhanda<alt:hand><t:at hand, available>|till havs<t:at/to sea>|krypa till kojs<t:go to bed>|kung<alt:till kungs><t:to the king>|till lags<t:of service, to please>|lands<alt:till lands><t:on land>|liv<alt:till livs><t:to life, to eat>|påsk<alt:till påska><t:until Easter>|redo<alt:till reds><t:to aid>|till råga på allt<t:to top it off, to make matters worse>|till salu<t:for sale>|till sjöss<t:at/to sea>|skog<alt:till skogs><t:at/to the forest>|till sängs<t:to bed>|torg<alt:till torgs><t:to the market>|till vardags<t:on weekdays>|till väders<t:up into the air>|gå till väga<t:go about>|ta till orda<t:start speaking>}}

