===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|tight}}, {{m|enm|tyght}}, {{m|enm|tyȝt}}, {{m|enm|tiht}}, variants of {{m|enm|thight}}, {{m|enm|thiht}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*þiht}}, {{m|ang|*þīht}} (attested in {{m|ang|meteþiht}}), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*þį̄ht(ī)}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*þinhtaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*tenkt-|t=dense, thick, tight}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ten-|t=to stretch, pull}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|ticht}}, {{cog|fy|ticht}}, {{cog|da|tæt}}, {{cog|is|þéttur|t=dense}}, {{cog|no|tett}}, {{cog|sv|tät}}, {{cog|nl|dicht|t=dense}}, {{cog|de|dicht|t=dense}}.

The current form with ''t-'' /t/ rather than etymologically-expected ''th-'' /θ/ arose in Middle English under the influence of the etymologically-unrelated verbs {{m|en|tighten}} and ''tight'', which come from a different Proto-Indo-European root (starting with *''d-'' and thus regularly having ''t-'').

